    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Trishear is a two-dimensional kinematic model of fault-propagation folding that has a rigidly moving hanging wall and a stationary foot wall, with a triangular shear zone ahead of the fault. This paper compares a trishear model and a boundary element (BEM) model for a case of a single slip event on a thrust fault with no fault tip propagation. Based on this study, trishear may not be appropriate for application in kinematic modeling of structure imaged by seismic reflection interpretion, but can be used as a first order approximation of how faults may influence folding. The physical significance of the trishear model needs to be investigated by further mechanical simulations.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
